#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="PreToolUse hook body: deny a verdict-bearing command whose exit status is discarded — by a pager/filter pipe, by a trailing list element, or by nohup/& — and a foreground sleep, which spends the session's turn"
#
# Shapes that report success while destroying the thing the harness consumes.
# All of them fail GREEN — exit 0 with plausible output — which is why noticing
# them repeatedly did not stop them.
#
#   piped-verdict   `mise run verify 2>&1 | tail -6` exits with tail's status,
#                   always 0. It produced two confident "green" reports over runs
#                   that had failed: once on a formatting check, once on a stale
#                   branch refusal.
#   trailing-list   `mise run verify >log 2>&1; echo "EXIT=$?"` reports the
#                   echo's status. No pipe involved, and it LOOKS compliant —
#                   this file used to prescribe it. Backgrounded it is worse than
#                   a misread: the task-completion notification carries the
#                   compound's status, so a failed task arrives as
#                   `completed (exit code 0)` — an authoritative statement from
#                   the harness rather than a reading of anyone's. Measured twice
#                   in one session, on `fmt` and then on `verify`.
#   orphaned-run    `nohup mise run land >log 2>&1 & sleep 3; echo started`
#                   returns immediately with the echo. The task is recorded
#                   complete, the work runs unsupervised, and the session loses
#                   its wake-up — leaving only polling or an idle turn, both
#                   forbidden, and an idle turn gets the VM reclaimed.
#   foreground-sleep `cd repo; sleep 90; git log --oneline -1` waits inside the
#                   call. It fails GREEN only up to ~2 minutes; past that the
#                   harness kills the call, so the poll that was meant to be
#                   patient FAILS instead (measured at exit 143 and 144). This
#                   one destroys the session rather than a verdict, and it is
#                   the shape the other three's root cause produces when there
#                   is nothing to read a status from at all.
#
# One root cause: treating a Bash call as a terminal that should print something
# short, when it is a supervised process whose exit status and lifetime are the
# interface. `| tail` makes the output small and drops the verdict; a trailing
# `; echo` replaces it; `nohup &` makes the call return fast and drops the
# supervision; a foreground `sleep` sits at the terminal waiting, which is the
# one thing a supervised process must never do.
#
# THE SCOPE FIX (CLOUD-199). This guard was scoped to the literal string `mise
# run`, and its own comment already stated the general rule it did not implement:
# a pipe replaces the command's exit status, which is a property of pipes, not of
# `mise`. Measured, minutes after it landed: it refused
# `git push … | tail -2; mise run module-map-check … | tail -2`, the agent re-ran
# the `git push … | tail -2` half ALONE and reported it as compliance, and that
# push had actually returned exit 1 (`stale info`). An agent reading a guard as a
# rule about one command string will comply with it exactly and keep making the
# error on the next one.
#
# A FILTER IS THE SAME SUBSTITUTION AS A PAGER. `cargo clippy … | grep -E
# '^error'` printed nothing and was reported clean; cargo colours its
# diagnostics, so every line begins with an ANSI escape and `^error` cannot match
# at line start. The filter was structurally incapable of finding what it
# searched for, and absence of matches was read as absence of errors. So the deny
# list carries filters alongside pagers, and the message states the PRINCIPLE —
# read the status from the harness — because complying with the narrower wording
# ("do not use a pager") is exactly how the next instance happened.
#
# What stays allowed, because it is the correct form: the command alone in the
# call, redirected to a file, with the file inspected in a SEPARATE call. A pager
# over a FILE is fine; a pager over a live task is not. Read-only queries
# (`git log`, `gh pr view`, `cargo metadata`) carry no verdict and compose
# freely, and `jq` is composition rather than a verdict substitute — widening to
# every pipe would deny ordinary work, and a guard with false positives gets
# bypassed.
#
# Fails OPEN on anything it cannot parse, and honours BATTEN_RUN_SHAPE_BYPASS=1.
set -uo pipefail

[ -n "${BATTEN_RUN_SHAPE_BYPASS:-}" ] && exit 0

raw=$(cat) || exit 0
cmd=$(printf '%s' "$raw" | jq -r '.tool_input.command // empty' 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$cmd" ] || exit 0

# --- the verdict-bearing table, written once as data (§1) --------------------
#
# A command is verdict-bearing when its exit status IS the answer the caller is
# asking for. Enumerable on purpose: an open predicate would be a judgement, and
# a closed list keeps this computable. The read-only members of each family are
# excluded by name, because a query's output is its answer and piping it is
# ordinary composition.
GIT_VERDICT='push|fetch|rebase'
GH_PR_VERDICT='create|ready|ready-for-review|merge|edit|close|reopen|comment'
CARGO_QUERY='metadata|tree|locate-project|pkgid|search|login|logout|version'
# Pagers and filters alike: each substitutes output for status.
FILTERS='tail|head|less|more|grep|rg|egrep|fgrep|awk|sed|wc'

# Heredoc BODIES are dropped first, then quoted spans neutralised, so a commit
# message, issue body or documentation paragraph *describing* these shapes is not
# judged as one. Quote-scrubbing alone is not enough: a heredoc writing prose that
# names `nohup`/`&` is unquoted text, and this guard denied exactly that on the
# command that documented it.
# A heredoc opener can appear anywhere on the line (`cat >> f <<EOF`), so this is
# not anchored — an anchored version missed `cat >> tests/x.bats <<BATS` and the
# guard denied the command writing its own test fixture. `<<<` is a here-STRING,
# not a heredoc, and must not start a skip that never terminates.
body=$(awk '
	!inheredoc && /<<-?[[:space:]]*['"'"'"]?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*['"'"'"]?/ && !/<<</ {
		line = $0
		sub(/^.*<<-?[[:space:]]*/, "", line)
		sub(/[^A-Za-z0-9_'"'"'"].*$/, "", line)
		gsub(/['"'"'"]/, "", line)
		if (line != "") { term = line; inheredoc = 1 }
		print; next
	}
	inheredoc { if ($0 ~ "^[[:space:]]*" term "[[:space:]]*$") inheredoc = 0; next }
	{ print }
' <<<"$cmd")
# -z, so a quoted span is neutralised across NEWLINES. sed is line-based by
# default, which left the interior of every multi-line `git commit -m "…"`
# exposed — and a commit message documenting these shapes is the single most
# likely place to write them down. The guard denied exactly that.
scrubbed=$(printf '%s' "$body" | sed -zE "s/'[^']*'/QUOTED/g; s/\"[^\"]*\"/QUOTED/g")

# --- resolve a pipeline stage to its EFFECTIVE program ------------------------
#
# Prints "<program> <first non-flag word> <second non-flag word>". The wrapper
# skip is `gh-guard-check`'s: in the web sandbox `mise exec -- …` is often the
# only working form, so a guard that stops at the wrapper token sees none of the
# calls that matter.
resolve() {
	local seg=$1
	local -a toks words
	read -r -a toks <<<"$seg"
	local count=${#toks[@]} i=0
	while [ "$i" -lt "$count" ] && [[ ${toks[$i]} =~ ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*= ]]; do i=$((i + 1)); done
	while :; do
		case "${toks[$i]:-}" in
		env | command | nice | stdbuf | timeout | xargs | sudo | doas | nohup)
			i=$((i + 1))
			while [ "$i" -lt "$count" ] && [[ ${toks[$i]} =~ ^(-|[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=|[0-9]) ]]; do i=$((i + 1)); done
			;;
		mise)
			# Only `mise exec`/`mise x` run another program; `mise run` names a
			# task and IS the thing being judged.
			case "${toks[$((i + 1))]:-}" in
			exec | x)
				i=$((i + 2))
				while [ "$i" -lt "$count" ] && [[ ${toks[$i]} =~ ^(-|[^ ]*@) ]]; do i=$((i + 1)); done
				;;
			*) break ;;
			esac
			;;
		*) break ;;
		esac
	done
	local prog=${toks[$i]:-}
	prog=${prog##*/}
	words=()
	local t
	for t in "${toks[@]:$((i + 1))}"; do
		case "$t" in -* | *'>'* | *'<'*) ;; *) words+=("$t") ;; esac
	done
	printf '%s %s %s' "$prog" "${words[0]:-}" "${words[1]:-}"
}

# Does this stage carry a verdict?
verdict_bearing() {
	local prog sub1 sub2
	read -r prog sub1 sub2 <<<"$(resolve "$1")"
	case "$prog" in
	mise) [ "$sub1" = run ] ;;
	git) [[ "$sub1" =~ ^($GIT_VERDICT)$ ]] ;;
	cargo) [ -n "$sub1" ] && ! [[ "$sub1" =~ ^($CARGO_QUERY)$ ]] ;;
	gh) [ "$sub1" = pr ] && [[ "$sub2" =~ ^($GH_PR_VERDICT)$ ]] ;;
	# `bats` (CLOUD-473). The omission was not an oversight about one runner: it
	# is every shell gate in this repo. `mise run test:bats` was covered by the
	# `mise` row, so the same suite was guarded through one spelling and open
	# through the other two — `mise exec -- bats …` (the web sandbox's working
	# form) and a path-invoked `tests/bats/bin/bats`. Measured: a suite piped to
	# `tail` reported green over three failures, one of them a hang.
	#
	# An argument is required because it is what makes the run a verdict: bare
	# `bats`, `bats --version` and `bats --help` print usage and answer nothing.
	# Flags are already stripped by `resolve`, so `$sub1` is the first file or
	# directory — or a `-f` filter's pattern, which is still a run.
	bats) [ -n "$sub1" ] ;;
	*) false ;;
	esac
}

# Is this stage a pager or filter — output standing in for status?
is_filter() {
	local prog
	read -r prog _ _ <<<"$(resolve "$1")"
	[[ "$prog" =~ ^($FILTERS)$ ]]
}

deny() {
	jq -n --arg r "$1" '{
    hookSpecificOutput: {
      hookEventName: "PreToolUse",
      permissionDecision: "deny",
      permissionDecisionReason: $r
    }
  }'
	exit 0
}

# The principle, not a command name. The previous wording named `mise run`, and
# an agent complied with it exactly while making the identical error on
# `git push` in the same session.
# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # the backticks are literal markdown, not a subshell
PRINCIPLE='A verdict-bearing command'"'"'s exit status must be READ FROM THE HARNESS, never inferred from its output. A pipe hands the status to the pager or filter; a following `;` or `||` list element replaces it with its own. Both report 0 over a failure.'

CORRECT='The compliant form is the command alone in the call:

  mise run <task> >/tmp/<task>.log 2>&1

The tool result IS the verdict — read the log in a SEPARATE call. Pass
run_in_background on the tool call itself for anything that can exceed ~2
minutes. A pager over a FILE is fine; a pager over a live task is not.'

# --- split into LIST elements, remembering the separator ----------------------
#
# `&&` and `||` are replaced before `;` so the two-character operators are never
# read as a bare `|` or `&`. What remains inside an element is a real pipe or a
# real background operator, which the two checks below judge.
list=$(printf '%s' "$scrubbed" | sed -E 's/\|\|/\n@OR@/g; s/\&\&/\n@AND@/g; s/;/\n@SEMI@/g')

elements=()
seps=()
while IFS= read -r line; do
	case "$line" in
	@OR@*)
		seps+=(OR)
		elements+=("${line#@OR@}")
		;;
	@AND@*)
		seps+=(AND)
		elements+=("${line#@AND@}")
		;;
	@SEMI@*)
		seps+=(SEMI)
		elements+=("${line#@SEMI@}")
		;;
	*)
		seps+=(FIRST)
		elements+=("$line")
		;;
	esac
done < <(printf '%s\n' "$list")

# --- foreground-sleep ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# A DIFFERENT FAMILY FROM THE THREE ABOVE, and it is here because it is the same
# root cause: treating a Bash call as a terminal you can wait at, when it is a
# supervised process whose lifetime is the interface. The other three destroy a
# verdict; this one destroys the SESSION.
#
# Measured 2026-08-12. A `git commit` hung inside a gate that had begun calling
# itself, and the session polled it with `sleep 90`, `sleep 100`, `sleep 180` in
# the foreground. A foreground call is killed at ~2 minutes, so the last two did
# not run slower — they FAILED, at exit 143 and 144 — and every turn in between
# bought nothing. The container was reclaimed with the fix uncommitted, and the
# branch it was landing is still a draft.
#
# AGENTS.md has stated "never use a foreground sleep" for as long as it has
# stated the other three, and nothing enforced it: this guard mentioned `sleep`
# only inside one comment's example. Prose is feedforward only (non-negotiable
# rule 2), and the session that hit this had read the prose.
#
# THE PREDICATE IS THE CALL'S OWN SHAPE, not the sleep's duration. A short sleep
# is not safer, it is the same shape spending less; and a background call's
# `until … sleep 0.5 … done` is the CORRECT form the tool documentation
# recommends, so `run_in_background` is what distinguishes them — the one fact
# about the call that is not in the command string. Judged per stage, so a sleep
# anywhere in a compound (`cd x; sleep 90; git log`, the exact measured shape)
# is caught rather than only a leading one.
if [ "$(printf '%s' "$raw" | jq -r '.tool_input.run_in_background // false' 2>/dev/null)" != true ]; then
	for element in "${elements[@]}"; do
		# `mapfile`, not `while read`: the last stage carries no trailing newline,
		# so a read-loop drops it — which for a one-stage element is every stage.
		mapfile -t sleep_stages < <(printf '%s' "$element" | tr '|' '\n')
		for stage in "${sleep_stages[@]}"; do
			read -r prog _ _ <<<"$(resolve "$stage")"
			[ "$prog" = sleep ] || continue
			deny "Refused: a foreground \`sleep\` spends the session's own turn waiting, and the call is killed at ~2 minutes — so a wait longer than that does not run slowly, it FAILS (measured: exit 143 and 144 over a hung commit, then the container was reclaimed with the work uncommitted).

Waiting is the harness's job, not the command's. Put the thing you are waiting for in the background — pass run_in_background on the tool call itself — and act on its exit; the harness re-invokes you. For a condition rather than a process, background a command that EXITS when the condition holds (\`until <test>; do sleep 1; done\`), which is a background wait and is allowed.

Never end a turn idle to watch something, and never poll in the foreground.

Bypass with BATTEN_RUN_SHAPE_BYPASS=1."
		done
	done
fi

# --- piped-verdict ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Judged per ELEMENT, not over the whole command string. A pager attached to the
# FIRST element says nothing about a later one, and judging the whole string
# denied exactly that — a correct command using the recommended form.
last_verdict=-1
index=0
for element in "${elements[@]}"; do
	[ -n "${element// /}" ] || {
		index=$((index + 1))
		continue
	}
	mapfile -t stages < <(printf '%s' "$element" | tr '|' '\n')
	if verdict_bearing "${stages[0]}"; then
		last_verdict=$index
		for stage in "${stages[@]:1}"; do
			if is_filter "$stage"; then
				deny "Refused: piping a verdict-bearing command into a pager or filter discards its exit status — the pipeline exits with the filter's, which is 0 whether the command passed or failed.

$PRINCIPLE

This has already produced \"green\" reports over a failed \`verify\`, a \`git push\` that returned \`stale info\` at exit 1, a \`cargo clippy\` whose ANSI-coloured output no \`^error\` anchor could ever match, and a \`cargo test\` whose tail window showed the last binaries' genuinely green blocks while an earlier one failed.

$CORRECT

Bypass with BATTEN_RUN_SHAPE_BYPASS=1."
			fi
		done
	fi
	index=$((index + 1))
done

# Nothing verdict-bearing anywhere: not this guard's business.
[ "$last_verdict" -ge 0 ] || exit 0

# --- orphaned-run -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `nohup`, or a trailing `&` that is not `&&`, detaches the command from the tool
# call. The call then returns immediately and the harness records it complete.
# File-descriptor redirections contain a literal `&` (`2>&1`, `&>log`, `>&2`)
# and are not background operators — the correct form uses one, so a naive `&`
# test denies exactly the shape this guard recommends. Strip them first, then a
# remaining `&` that is not `&&` is a real detach.
no_redir=$(sed -E 's/[0-9]*>&[0-9-]+//g; s/&>>?//g' <<<"$scrubbed")
if grep -qE '(^|[^[:alnum:]_-])nohup([[:space:]]|$)' <<<"$no_redir" ||
	grep -qE '(^|[^&])&[[:space:]]*($|[^&])' <<<"$no_redir"; then
	deny "Refused: detaching a verdict-bearing command with nohup or \`&\` orphans it from the tool call. The call returns immediately, the harness records it complete, and the session loses the wake-up it would get when the work actually exits — leaving only polling or an idle turn, both of which the workflow contract forbids.

Pass run_in_background on the tool call itself and let the command run in the foreground of that call; the harness re-invokes you on exit.

$CORRECT

Bypass with BATTEN_RUN_SHAPE_BYPASS=1."
fi

# --- trailing-list -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only the LAST element's status survives a `;` or `||` chain, so a verdict-bearing
# command with either after it has had its verdict replaced.
#
# `&&` IS NOT DENIED, and this is a deliberate departure from CLOUD-199's written
# acceptance, which named all three operators. `a && b` short-circuits: when `a`
# fails, `b` never runs and the list exits with `a`'s non-zero status. There is no
# false green to stop, so denying it would be a pure false positive — and the same
# issue argues that a guard with false positives gets bypassed, and a bypassed
# guard enforces nothing. `verify`'s own body is built from guarded `if ! cmd`
# chains for exactly the fail-closed property `&&` provides. `;` and `||` both
# genuinely discard the verdict, and every instance measured on this issue was a
# `;`.
for ((k = last_verdict + 1; k < ${#elements[@]}; k++)); do
	case "${seps[$k]}" in
	SEMI | OR) ;;
	*) continue ;;
	esac
	[ -n "${elements[$k]// /}" ] || continue
	deny "Refused: a verdict-bearing command followed by \`;\` or \`||\` in the same Bash list has its exit status replaced — only the last element's survives.

$PRINCIPLE

This is the laundered shape: it looks compliant, and this guard used to recommend it. Backgrounded it is worse than a misread — the task-completion notification carries the compound's status, so a failed task arrives as \`completed (exit code 0)\`, an authoritative-looking statement from the harness. Measured twice in one session, on \`fmt\` and then on \`verify\`.

(\`&&\` is fine: it short-circuits, so a failure still propagates.)

$CORRECT

Bypass with BATTEN_RUN_SHAPE_BYPASS=1."
done

exit 0
